  "numerical_characteristics": {
    "dtype": null,
    "precision": null,
    "magnitude": null,
    "tolerance": null,
    "category_specific_evidence": [
      "PR #3246 states the analytical gradient of sigma_max now perfectly matches the numerical gradient to machine precision for non-square matrices.",
      "The root cause is LA.svd(values[0]) in sigma_max._grad() using full_matrices=True by default, producing full unitary matrices with misaligned inner dimensions for rectangular matrices.",
      "The fix changes the call to LA.svd(values[0], full_matrices=False) for economy-size SVD, matching a prior fix to normNuc in PR #2426.",
      "The gradient is U diag(e_1) V.T, i.e. the gradient of the largest singular value, with ds[0]=1 and zeros elsewhere.",
      "New test configs were added in test_grad.py for sigma_max on shapes (2, 3) and (4, 2) with unrestricted domain.",
      "Reported symptom is a ValueError crash when calling .grad on sigma_max with non-square matrices, not a silently wrong numerical value in the passages."
    ],
    "reproduction_risks": [
      "Passages describe a crash (ValueError) as the primary symptom; no concrete wrong numerical gradient values are given to check against.",
      "Machine-precision tolerance is described qualitatively only; no numeric tolerance is stated.",
      "No concrete input matrices or expected gradient values are provided beyond shapes (2, 3) and (4, 2) in the test diff."
    ]
  },
